.TH imperaterm 1 "November 2026" "imperaterm 1.0.0"
.ss 12 0
.SH NAME
imperaterm \- a terminal emulator for CGTK
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B imperaterm
.RB [ \-\-separate ]
.RB [ \-e
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B imperaterm
is a terminal emulator with tabs, a scrollback and selections, drawn with
OpenGL. To the programs that run in it it is an xterm:
.B TERM
is set to xterm\-256color and
.B COLORTERM
to truecolor.
.PP
All the windows on a display are one
.BR imperaterm .
One that is started while another runs hands that one what it was to run,
with its directory and its environment, has a window of it, and waits
until the tab that came of it is gone.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-e " command"
Run the command, with the arguments that follow it, in place of the shell.
This is the last option: everything after it is the command's.
.TP
.B \-\-separate
Be an
.B imperaterm
of its own, which joins none and which none joins. One that is given an
option of GTK's is on its own too.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the options and exit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+T
New tab, in the directory of the current one.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+N
New window.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+W
Close the tab.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+Q
Close the window.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Page Up, Ctrl+Page Down
The previous and the next tab. With Shift, the tab is moved.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+C, Ctrl+Shift+V
Copy the selection to the clipboard, and paste the clipboard.
.TP
.B Shift+Insert
Paste the primary selection.
.TP
.B Shift+Page Up, Shift+Page Down, Shift+Home, Shift+End
Scroll through the scrollback.
.TP
.B Ctrl++, Ctrl+\-, Ctrl+0
Larger and smaller text, and the size as set.
.SH TABS
A tab says who is at the terminal and where, as user@host: directory,
or what a program sets as the title. The right button on a tab has a menu;
Rename Tab in it, or a double click on the tab, gives the tab a name of
your own until it is closed. An empty name gives it back its own.
A tab dragged along the bar changes places with those it passes.
Dragged off the bar it is carried: dropped on another window it goes
there, and dropped on none it has a new window of its own.
Dropped back on its own window it stays where it was.
Tabs at the bottom in Edit > Preferences puts the tabs under the terminal.
.PP
Who is at the terminal and where is what the shell says at each prompt,
with OSC 7 (file://host/directory) and OSC 1337 (RemoteHost=user@host).
.I imperaterm.sh
has a shell say it. The shell
.B imperaterm
starts reads it unasked, by
.B PROMPT_COMMAND
or
.B ENV
in its environment where those are not set already; login shells, as
after sudo \-i, su \- or ssh, read it if it is in /etc/profile.d. A shell
that says nothing leaves the tab as it was. The README has the rest.
.SH MOUSE
Dragging with the left button selects; a double click selects a word and
a triple click a line. With Control held the selection is a block. Shift
and a click move the end of the selection. The middle button pastes the
primary selection and the right button opens a menu. The wheel scrolls.
When a program has asked for the mouse, hold Shift to select.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B IMPERATERM_GLES
If set, OpenGL ES 3.0 is used even where OpenGL 3.1 is there.
.TP
.B SHELL
The shell to run. Without it, the user's shell from the password file.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I imperaterm.sh
In the imperaterm directory under share, and in profile.d under the
configuration directory Imperaterm was built for: what a shell reads to tell
the terminal who is at it and where.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/imperaterm/imperatermrc
The settings, as Edit > Preferences keeps them.
.I ~/.config
stands in for
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
when that is not set.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/imperaterm\-*
The socket at which an
.B imperaterm
that is started finds the one that runs, and the lock beside it. There is
one for each display. The directory must be the user's alone; where it is
not, or is not set, each
.B imperaterm
is its own.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xterm (1)
